#edb/ref 17592186173144 :tutorial/content "Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (on the left) is position number $0.$\n\nWe can write a value in Pascal's triangle as $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}},$ where the value is located in row $\\color{red}a$ and position ${\\color{blue}b}.$\nThis is known as a **binomial coefficient**.\nIn words, we say $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}}$ as \"$\\color{red}{a}$ choose ${\\color{blue}b}$\".\n\nFor example, the value located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle{{\\color{red}4} \\choose \\color{blue}{2}} = 6.$ Remember, we start counting from zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "62d4b142-a86a-52c9-b45a-1153afedf865" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3393 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "133af8ac-b2ce-5603-9334-ce3da1e8d068" :step/math-academy-id 18977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171810 :question/id #uuid "9f9efb07-33a0-5a28-8eef-656a3c00a200" :question/math-academy-id "e-5231"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5c108c0-f32a-5725-af3b-c3e747da09f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338779}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Binomial Coefficients Using the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "0b7576cb-372f-5a6a-ad5b-4f0b958a39e3" :step/math-academy-id 19269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173146 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wanted to calculate the value of $\\displaystyle{30 \\choose 3}.$ If we were to use Pascal's triangle, we'd have to write out $31$ rows, which would take a long time. Thankfully, there is a formula for calculating binomial coefficients. \n\nThe binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b89d1a7e-10c1-5ec2-b745-c3c45e0d7814" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3893 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Binomial Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b2f96998-bbd7-586b-b0d8-4cdbe2c42034" :step/math-academy-id 23007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172766}}] :activity/title "Pascal's Triangle and the Binomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172193}]}
